**End of Listing 10.19.** The classifier submodel is completed by looping over the layer names `"pooler"` and `"predictions"`, fetching each with `model.get_layer(layer_name)` and applying it to `x`. `classifier_model = keras.Model(classifier_input, x)` now maps last-conv activations to class probabilities.

**Computing the gradient.** Next we need the gradient of the top predicted class for our input image with respect to the activations of the last convolution layer. "Once again" (as for filter visualization in section 10.2), computing gradients means using **backend APIs**.

**10.3.1 TensorFlow version (Listing 10.20).** `get_top_class_gradients(img_array)`:

1. computes `last_conv_layer_output = last_conv_layer_model(img_array)`;
2. opens a `tf.GradientTape()` and calls `tape.watch(last_conv_layer_output)`, because this is a plain tensor, not a trainable variable;
3. runs `preds = classifier_model(last_conv_layer_output)` inside the tape;
4. finds `top_pred_index = ops.argmax(preds[0])` and slices `top_class_channel = preds[:, top_pred_index]`, the activation channel of the top predicted class;
5. calls `tape.gradient(top_class_channel, last_conv_layer_output)` to get the gradient of that class with respect to the feature map, and returns it together with the feature map.

The function is called on the preprocessed `img_array`, and both results are converted with `ops.convert_to_numpy` so the heatmap can be computed with NumPy (p.328).

**10.3.2 PyTorch version (Listing 10.21, begins).** The PyTorch version uses `.backward()` and `.grad`. The page shows only the first two lines: the function header and the computation of the last conv activations; the rest is on p.327.

